function COMPONENT(request : dval) {
    var page := dval({source_page: ""})
    page["source_page"] = "capy-07-collections-and-records"
    page["kind"] = "guide"
    page["slug"] = "collections-and-records"
    page["route"] = "/doc/guide/collections-and-records/"
    page["title"] = "7. Arrays, tuples, and structs"
    page["content"] = "Use an array for an ordered collection of one type. Use a tuple for a short fixed group. Use a struct when fields need names.\n\n## Arrays\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nAda\n\n```\n\nArray indexes start at zero. An invalid index traps.\n\nDeclare the item type when an array starts empty:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(\"Ada\")\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`push`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove`, `clear`, `reserve`, and `resize` change an array.\n\n## Array values\n\nAn array is a mutable reference value inside one workspace:\n\n```capy\nfunction append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], \" \", second[2], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAssignment and parameter passing share the array identity. Returns and captures also share it. Content mutation through a parameter affects the caller. A parameter binding remains immutable, so `values = [3]` is invalid. Array growth preserves identity.\n\nA `for` loop reads array items in order:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe optional index follows the value and has type `s32`. The loop rechecks the current array length. It safely stops after shrinkage and can visit appended items. The loop retains each current managed item while the body runs.\n\nThe loop item is a value, not an assignable array position. Use an index to replace an item.\n\n## Tuples\n\nA tuple is immutable. It has fixed positions and can contain different types:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(re"
    page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "quest : dval) {\n    var result := (200, \"ok\")\n    print(result[0], \" \", result[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`(value)` only groups an expression. It does not create a one-item tuple.\n\nA tuple can spread into matching function parameters:\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -> left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Structs\n\nA struct defines a named fixed layout:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe generated constructor takes fields in declaration order. A struct instance is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity. Struct fields are assignable:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe field mutation affects the caller. The parameter binding itself remains immutable.\n\nA receiver-first function can use method syntax:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -> point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis is the same call as `total(point)`.\n\n## Choose the right shape\n\nUse an array when every item has one static type. Use a tuple for a small fixed return value. Use a struct for application data with a stable shape. Use a `dval` map or list for decoded input and data that crosses a Bearer boundary.\n"
    page["label"] = "7. Arrays, tuples, and structs"
    page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
    page["param_lines"] = dval([])
    page["notes"] = dval([])
    page["warnings"] = dval([])
    page["see"] = dval([{label: "3. Types", href: "/doc/guide/values-and-types/"}, {label: "5. Functions", href: "/doc/guide/functions-and-closures/"}, {label: "8. Dynamic values", href: "/doc/guide/dynamic-values/"}, {label: "length", href: "/doc/api/length/"}, {label: "set", href: "/doc/api/set/"}])
    page["examples"] = dval([])
    page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Ada\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(\"Ada\")\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], \" \", second[2], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := (200, \"ok\")\n    print(result[0], \" \", result[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -> left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -> point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
    component_render("/doc/components/doc_page.capy", page)
}
